{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fjj9AdXJGU4JhnfjK8WhKq","type":"tool_result","content":"EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739\nEurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739.html\nPLAN.md\nSTAGE.md\nTASK.md\nexit-interview.json\n{\n\"date\": \"2026-11-04 07:17:39\",\n\"source\": \"European football (over the top arcade style)\",\n\"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n\"genre\": \"Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (four outfield players and a keeper a side) wi","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452d6482-9020-4e99-982f-14f7a7dcf527","uuid":"1bea7ccd-0985-40fe-be6f-9b4902662101","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:23:22.640Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739\nEurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739.html\nPLAN.md\nSTAGE.md\nTASK.md\nexit-interview.json\n{\n\"date\": \"2026-11-04 07:17:39\",\n\"source\": \"European football (over the top arcade style)\",\n\"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n\"genre\": \"Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (four outfield players and a keeper a side) wi","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Fjj9AdXJGU4JhnfjK8WhKq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiCNcMsQyEcRc1Y3R7Tb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Europhoria Football**, an over-the-top arcade 5-a-side football game, as the single file `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739.html` in the work root. It runs from `file://` with nothing loaded from the internet. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and fills every field. The final run of `node tools/test.mjs` passed all 498 checks. That includes a scripted playthrough of the whole game, a browser pass that draws every scene and moment, and the AI Hub's own harness in all four of its scenarios.\n\n**What you can play:**\n- **Cup Run:** a broke seaside club gets into Europe's biggest cup by a typo. A prologue, five rounds in five stadiums, and a bribe offer from the villain before the final. Between matches there are tabloid back pages, a travel map, team talks and an optional challenge each round. Losing gives a 10-second CONTINUE?. Winning the final plays a trophy ceremony, a harbour parade and credits.\n- **Kick-About:** six teams, each with its own tactics and gimmick (one team literally parks a bus in front of its goal when leading late). Five stadiums with weather, and 1–4 local players on either side or together.\n- **Penalties**, **Training** (nine coached drills) and a **Sticker Album** of 51 stickers.\n- **The over-the-top layer:** the crowd is your power bar. When it fills you trigger Europhoria and get one Super Strike. If a human is in goal, it becomes a timed Save Duel. There are also dives, VAR, cards, replays, celebrations and random matchday events.\n- **Presentation:** two-voice commentary (about 330 lines) and all audio made in code: songs, a reactive crowd that sings chants, and gibberish voices.\n\n**What else TASK.md asked for:**\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 s idle on the title or menus, and any input exits.\n- **Dev mode:** reachable from the pause menu (or `` ` ``/F1), with live sliders, reset-to-defaults, cheats and warps.\n- **Controls:** PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in Settings, and prompts follow the last device. Touch-only devices get a \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice and a Settings button.\n- **About & Credits:** in-game pages built from the exit interview, so the two always match.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- The title is the plan's (Europhoria Football); I didn't pick again. File names follow TASK.md, since the plan's names were from another run.\n- I changed the engine in five small places (babbling voices, audio buses, football kits with numbers, a whole-body tilt for dives, accented letters). They're listed in `engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md`.\n- I never heard the audio. A browser check ran every sound, chant and voice without errors, but whether it sounds good is untested.\n- Gameplay balance was only tuned against AI-vs-AI matches (about 2.3 goals per match at short halves), not by playing it.\n- I deleted the engine and hub clones from the dev folder so no nested git repos are left. Nothing was committed.\n\n---\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:17:39\n- **Source:** European football (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (four outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run, Kick-About, Penalties and Training. A TV broadcast camera: side-on, raised 40 degrees, scrolling with play (a custom engine View). Classic three-quarter and Aerial top-down are in Settings, and replays use turned cameras.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, full engine, five small changes listed in engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md). Its mocap player, with a 31-clip set cut from Quaternius, Mesh2Motion and CMU. The AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Plain JavaScript (ES2022) in one IIFE, built from 40 source fragments into a single HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** my-3D2dge's native PS1-era pixel art (ps1 resolution, expanded to any screen). HD Humanoid rigs in team kits with hoops, stripes, halves, sashes and shirt numbers. Textured TileMap stadiums with tiered stands, code-drawn crowds, LED boards, floodlight pylons, nets that ripple and a skyline per city. Crests, tabloid pages, a travel map and stickers are painted in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid rig (gait from velocity, attack specs for passes, shots, volleys, pokes and step-overs, poses and faces). Mocap clips through Mocap.drive (slide tackles, celebrations, throws, get-ups, dodges). A new whole-body tilt for keeper dives, diving headers, bicycle kicks, launched players and theatrical dives.\n- **Audio:** All in code, no audio files. The engine's chip synth for about 40 effects and 16 original songs (the Europhoria anthem and its variations, and Europhoria layers that come in during play). A WebAudio crowd engine (a noise bed that follows the mood, OOOHs, roars, boos, whistles, claps) with team chants sung by an 8-voice formant choir. Animalese-style babble voices for the two commentators, the cast and the echoing stadium PA.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 2–4 players. A host-authoritative match, snapshots 14 a second and grouped events 5 a second, client interpolation, the game's own card (the tunnel walkout), server mode, leaving, time-up and finish. It passes the hub harness's automatic check in all four scenarios.\n- **Testing:** One command, `node tools/test.mjs`, with 17 suites that print only failures and a count:\n  - unit physics, rules, Euphoria and super strikes, AI with a balance soak, content\n  - UI by pad, keyboard, mouse and touch-only; saves; attract mode; dev mode; all nine training drills\n  - determinism, lint, and a full scripted playthrough of the cup and every mode\n  - a fake-hub loopback with latency, jitter and loss\n  - a browser sweep that draws every scene and moment, and browser checks with the engine's checker at three screen sizes\n  - the real AI Hub harness in four scenarios\n\n  498 checks, all passing. Screenshots were used only for looks.\n- **Research:** Read the engine's handoff docs (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md and API-INDEX.md), the SPORTS kit, the template page and test, the mocap player's header and clip catalogs, and the AI Hub README, harness and session client. No web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Session time about 2 hours 10 minutes (07:17 to about 09:27). Token count and API cost unknown (no usage meter was available to this session).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 34: 24 in the game and 10 in the tests themselves. The game's were:\n  - players frozen on their set-piece spots after every restart\n  - keepers who never saved corner-bound shots\n  - a crash when red cards left a side with no outfield players\n  - three crashes at load or on entering a scene (a registry used before it existed, front-end scenes losing their own methods, story data read before it was defined)\n  - two training drills that crashed looking up a hidden keeper\n  - a draw-time crash in the attract mode's scripted Save Duel\n  - humans auto-switched onto their own goalkeeper\n  - passes that under-led moving receivers\n  - a team with no captain, two short commentary pools, and Math.random in the simulation\n  - two syntax errors in commentary text\n  - screen problems: the PA box over the score and the notices, set-piece prompts and the replay tag under the commentary, the logo over the menu, unknown glyphs, a washed-out snow pitch, and the results over the LED boards\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 8: the frozen players, the red-card crash, the three load and scene crashes, the two broken training drills (counted as one), and the two syntax errors that would have stopped the game from loading at all.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The AI-versus-AI smoke run in Node, and the scripted playthrough that grew out of it. Seeded matches for every tactic printed goals, shots, passes, fouls and a stuck-ball counter. That found the frozen players, the keeper balance and the red-card crash within minutes. The playthrough then drove the whole cup, the ending, Kick-About, Penalties, a drill, About and the attract mode through the real input.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Still screenshots of the super strike. Timing a browser screenshot from outside the page to a 0.6-second slow-motion charge-up gave confusing frames. The headless test that every super path reaches the goal answered the real question. The opposite lesson: headless tests skip drawing, so the browser sweep that draws every moment was worth adding late. It caught the attract mode's draw-time crash.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** It runs headless by default with exact fixed steps. The same `game.advance` drives unit tests, a full playthrough of the game in under two minutes, and four networked copies in lockstep. And the Humanoid rig gives dressed, shaded characters with kick specs, poses, faces and portraits for free.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n  - E.store prefixes its keys, and a test read localStorage directly.\n  - tools/check.mjs can't take a `?query`, so I added a `#touchonly` deep link.\n  - Clips cut with a `SET:` prefix lose it in the set.\n  - Canvas lighting with floodlights makes a night pitch read as day until the ambient is lowered.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The football itself: a match simulation (ball physics with woodwork and nets, footballers, keepers, team AI, rules, set pieces, the referee, Euphoria). The presentation layer only reads its state and events, so replays, bots, tests, the attract mode and the network host all use the same code path.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Depth and framing in a custom 40-degree view. The goal nets, the LED board faces and the tifo had to be queued at the right anchors to sort against players. And the camera's zoom stacks the scene's start, the player's zoom and the director's own.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Five changes, all marked `// EF:` and listed in CHANGES-ENGINE.md:\n  - dialog voices that can be functions (a babble syllable per letter)\n  - named audio buses, with `now()` and the noise buffer\n  - football kit patterns and shirt numbers on the Humanoid\n  - a whole-body tilt state for dives and acrobatics\n  - accented letters in the 5x7 font\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A built-in tilt or orientation channel on the Humanoid for dives, falls and acrobatics, together with kit patterns. Then sports and action games wouldn't have to patch the rig.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It's concrete about the deliverable and the test discipline (one command, only failures, no subagents judging) and leaves the genre, look and scope to the builder. The attached plan made the size of the game explicit.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run: its file and folder names carry a different model, effort and date, so the names had to be re-derived from TASK.md. The plan also asks for more than TASK.md does, so how much of it was required had to be judged.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting a whole sports game, a story mode, training, the attract mode, dev tools and networked play into one coherent single-file build where every feature is real and tested.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Tie the plan's file names to TASK.md's run name (or mark them as placeholders), and say whether a plan's extra scope is required or optional."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1069,"cache_read_input_tokens":901245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1069},"output_tokens":743,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452d6482-9020-4e99-982f-14f7a7dcf527","uuid":"882e7f1a-c419-4669-9bc0-0fbd1fe883f7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:23:55.226Z","request_id":"req_011CgiCNbx4CeoZj3oAWcLAF"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not all conditions are evidenced. The bundled HTML exists at the work root and exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the final reply contains all four sections. However, the file names do not match TASK.md's required names: TASK.md asks for [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739.html] and a dev subfolder named [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739], but the transcript shows the deliverable named EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739.html, which differs from the plan's name in the same way the TASK wording does. Also, the transcript explicitly says the title and file name were taken from PLAN.md rather than TASK.md, and that 'the plan asks for more than TASK.md does' with its required scope never confirmed, so 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is asserted but not verified against TASK.md line by line. Finally, the reply notes the audio was never heard and balance was never played, leaving quality points unverified, and the TASK.md requirement that dev files live in a subfolder with that name is only partly met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452d6482-9020-4e99-982f-14f7a7dcf527","uuid":"af9e0ddf-7431-4557-9618-7c4f50954082","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:24:03.622Z","isSynthetic":true}
