{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiGgUqQXR1Wgg4zWQxGf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Europhoria Football is finished: the final full test run on the final build passed all 526 checks with none failing.\n\nThe earlier run you asked about had one failure, a flaky Save Duel test. The quick-jump to the duel didn't fix the random seed, and sometimes a defender tackled the shooter before the super strike happened. I fixed that by clearing nearby defenders and pinning the test's seed.\n\n**Points the review raised:**\n- **File names.** TASK.md's names are templates. Filling in GameTitle = \"Europhoria Football\" and SOURCE = \"European football\" gives `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-071739`. That's the name of both the `.html` file in the work root and the dev folder.\n- **Where files live.** Every development file is inside that dev folder. The work root holds only the three given files (TASK, PLAN, STAGE), the `.html` and `exit-interview.json`, as TASK.md asks.\n- **The title.** TASK.md asks for 50 brainstormed titles and a random pick. The plan did exactly that (`secrets.randbelow(50)` chose index 0), and STAGE.md says to use the plan's title, so the two don't conflict.\n- **Every TASK.md feature.** `TASK-CHECKLIST.md` in the dev folder goes through TASK.md line by line, giving where each requirement is built and the test that proves it. There are no TODOs, placeholders or stubs (a lint test checks every source file).\n- **Audio.** I still haven't heard it, but it is now measured. In Chromium, every effect, song, Europhoria layer, crowd reaction, chant and voice registers at the master output (peaks about 0.1–0.33). Silence reads as silent, and setting the Voices or Crowd slider to 0 silences those sounds. Crowd roars were about three times louder than everything else, so I turned them down.\n- **Balance.** A new test plays a bot-controlled human side against the CPU, 12 matches per difficulty. That test found a real bug: difficulty also changed the human's own teammates, so it barely made a difference. With that fixed and CPU speed added per difficulty, the human side's average goal difference per match is +0.5 on Easy, −0.25 on Normal and −0.83 on Hard. No person has played it.\n- **Dev mode and the mouse.** Pressing Escape and clicking \"Dev mode\" in the real pause menu opens the panel over the running match. Moving the mouse turns on aiming, a left click passes toward the cursor, and holding then releasing the right button shoots.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:17:39\n- **Source:** European football (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (four outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run, Kick-About, Penalties and Training. The camera is a TV broadcast view, side-on and raised 40 degrees, scrolling with play. Classic three-quarter and Aerial top-down are in Settings, and replays use turned cameras.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, full engine, five small changes listed in `engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md`).\n  - Its motion-capture player, with 31 clips cut from Quaternius, Mesh2Motion and CMU.\n  - The AI Hub session client 1.4.0.\n  - Plain JavaScript (ES2022) in one script, built from 40 source files into a single HTML file.\n  - Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** my-3D2dge's native PS1-era pixel art, expanded to fill any screen.\n  - Characters in team kits with hoops, stripes, halves, sashes and shirt numbers.\n  - Textured stadiums with tiered stands, crowds drawn in code, LED boards, floodlight pylons, nets that ripple and a skyline per city.\n  - Crests, tabloid pages, a travel map and stickers painted in code.\n- **Animation:**\n  - The engine's procedural character rig: walking and running from velocity, kick motions for passes, shots, volleys, pokes and step-overs, poses and faces.\n  - Motion-capture clips for slide tackles, celebrations, throws, get-ups and dodges.\n  - A new whole-body tilt for keeper dives, diving headers, bicycle kicks, launched players and theatrical dives.\n- **Audio:** All in code, no audio files.\n  - The engine's synth for about 40 effects and 16 original songs, including the Europhoria anthem and music layers that come in during play.\n  - A crowd engine that follows the mood (OOOHs, roars, boos, whistles, claps) and sings team chants with an 8-voice choir.\n  - Animalese-style babble voices for the two commentators, the cast and the echoing stadium PA.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 2–4 players.\n  - The host runs the match and sends snapshots 14 times a second and grouped events 5 times a second; clients interpolate.\n  - The game shows its own card (the tunnel walkout) and supports server mode, players leaving, time-up and finish.\n  - It passes the hub harness's automatic check in all four scenarios.\n- **Testing:** One command, `node tools/test.mjs`, with 18 suites that print only failures and a count (526 checks). Screenshots were used only for looks.\n  - Unit physics, rules, Euphoria and super strikes, content, saves, determinism and lint.\n  - AI, with a balance run and the difficulty curve described above.\n  - Controls and menus by pad, keyboard, mouse and touch-only; the attract mode; dev mode; all nine training drills.\n  - A full scripted playthrough of the cup and every mode.\n  - A simulated hub with latency, jitter and packet loss.\n  - In Chromium:\n    - a sweep that draws every scene and moment\n    - the audio and input checks described above\n    - the engine's checker at three screen sizes\n    - the real AI Hub harness in four scenarios\n- **Research:** Read the engine's handoff docs (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md and API-INDEX.md), the SPORTS kit, the template page and test, the motion-capture player's header and clip catalogs, and the AI Hub README, harness and session client. No web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Session time about 3 hours 10 minutes (07:17 to about 10:27). Token count and API cost unknown (no usage meter was available to this session).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37: 25 in the game and 12 in the tests themselves. The game's were:\n  - players frozen on their set-piece spots after every restart\n  - keepers who never saved shots aimed at the corners\n  - a crash when red cards left a side with no outfield players\n  - three crashes at load or on entering a scene\n  - two training drills that crashed looking up a hidden keeper\n  - a crash while drawing the attract mode's scripted Save Duel\n  - difficulty that also changed the human's own teammates, so it barely did anything\n  - humans auto-switched onto their own goalkeeper\n  - passes that didn't lead moving teammates far enough\n  - a team with no captain, two short commentary pools, and the simulation using an unseeded random source\n  - two syntax errors in commentary text\n  - screen-layout problems, such as the PA box over the score and prompts hidden under the commentary\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 8: the frozen players, the red-card crash, the three load and scene crashes, the two broken training drills (counted as one), and the two syntax errors that would have stopped the game loading.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The AI-versus-AI run in Node and the scripted playthrough that grew out of it. Seeded matches for every tactic found the frozen players, the keeper balance and the red-card crash within minutes. The playthrough then drove the whole cup, the ending, every mode and the attract mode through the real controls.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Still screenshots of the super strike. Timing a screenshot to a 0.6-second slow-motion moment gave confusing frames, while the headless test that every super path reaches the goal answered the real question. The opposite lesson: headless tests skip drawing, so adding a browser pass that draws every moment was worth it late on. It caught the attract-mode crash.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** It runs without a screen by default, in exact fixed steps. The same stepping drives unit tests, a full playthrough in under two minutes, and four networked copies in lockstep. The character rig gives dressed, shaded characters with kicks, poses, faces and portraits for free.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n  - The engine's save storage adds a prefix to its keys, and one test read the browser's storage directly.\n  - The engine's checker can't take a `?query`, so I added a `#touchonly` link.\n  - Clips cut with a set prefix lose it.\n  - Floodlit night pitches read as day until the ambient light is lowered.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The football itself: ball physics with woodwork and nets, footballers, keepers, team AI, rules, set pieces, the referee and Euphoria. The drawing and sound only read its state and events. That way replays, bots, tests, the attract mode and the network host all run the same code.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Depth sorting and framing in a custom 40-degree view. The goal nets, LED board faces and the stadium banner had to be anchored carefully to sort correctly against players, and three separate zoom levels multiply together.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Five changes, all marked `// EF:` and listed in `CHANGES-ENGINE.md`:\n  - dialog voices that can be functions, for a babble syllable per letter\n  - named audio channels, so voices and crowd get their own volume settings\n  - football kit patterns and shirt numbers on the character rig\n  - a whole-body tilt for dives and acrobatics\n  - accented letters in the pixel font\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A built-in tilt or orientation channel on the character rig, plus kit patterns, so sports and action games don't have to patch it.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It is concrete about what to deliver and how to test (one command, only failures, no subagents judging), and leaves genre, look and scope to the builder. The attached plan made the size of the game explicit.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run: its file and folder names carry a different model, effort and date, so the names had to be re-derived from TASK.md. The plan also asks for more than TASK.md does, so how much of it was required had to be judged.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting a whole sports game, a story mode, training, the attract mode, dev tools and online play into one coherent single-file build where every feature is real and tested.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Tie the plan's file names to TASK.md's run name, or mark them as placeholders, and say whether the plan's extra scope is required or optional."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2489,"cache_read_input_tokens":946760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2489},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452d6482-9020-4e99-982f-14f7a7dcf527","uuid":"8d015a81-69c3-409b-9944-b626b58078df","timestamp":"2026-11-04T17:20:24.105Z","request_id":"req_011CgiGgUUpFWdFGSsEXPtfG"}
