{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GwQRZ6RZbpi4ZZrMZKicEp","type":"tool_result","content":"animation => The engine's procedural Humanoid rig (gait from velocity, attack specs for passes, shots, volleys, pokes and step-overs, poses and faces), mocap clips through Mocap.drive (slide tackles, celebrations, throws, get-ups, dodges), and a new whole-body tilt for keeper dives, diving headers, bicycle kicks, launched players and theatrical dives\n\ntesting => One command, node tools/test.mjs, 18 suites printing only failures and a count: unit physics, rules, Euphoria and super strikes, AI with a balance soak and a difficulty curve (a bot-driven human side against the CPU at Easy, Normal and Hard), content, UI by pad, keyboard, mouse and touch-only, saves, attract mode, dev mode, all nine training drills, determinism, lint, a full scripted playthrough of the cup and every mode, a fake-hub loopback with latency, jitter and loss, a browser sweep that draws every scene and moment, an audio and input check in Chromium (every kind of sound measured at the master output, the volume sliders, the pause menu's Dev mode, mouse aim, click to pass and shoot), browser checks with the engine's checker at three screen sizes, and the real AI Hub harness in four scenarios; screenshots only for looks (526 checks)\n\nusage => Session time about 3 hours 10 minutes (07:17 to about 10:27); token count and API cost unknown (no usage meter was available to this session)\n\nT 0 About 37: 25 in the game and 12 in the tests themselves. The game's: players frozen on their set-piece spots after every restart, keepers who never saved corner-bound shots, a crash when red cards left a side with no outfield players, three crashes at load or on entering a scene (a registry used before it existed, front-end scenes losing their own methods, story data read before it was defined), two training drills that crashed looking up a hidden keeper, a draw-time crash in the attract mode's scripted Save Duel, difficulty that also weakened or strengthened the human's own teammates (so it barely changed anything), humans auto-switched onto their own goalkeeper, passes that under-led moving receivers, a team with no captain, two short commentary pools, Math.random in the simulation, two syntax errors in commentary text, and screen problems (the PA box over the score and the notices, set-piece prompts and the replay tag under the commentary, the logo over the menu, unknown glyphs, a washed-out snow pitch, the results over the LED boards).\n\nT 1 8 were critical: the frozen players, the red-card crash, the three load and scene crashes, the two broken training drills (counted as one), and the two syntax errors that would have stopped the game from loading at all.\n\nT 2 The AI-versus-AI smoke run in Node and the scripted playthrough that grew out of it: seeded matches for every tactic printing goals, shots, passes, fouls and a stuck-ball counter found the frozen players, the keeper balance and the red-card crash within minutes; the playthrough then drove the whole cup, the ending, Kick-About, Penalties, a drill, About and the attract mode through the real input.\n\nT 3 Still screenshots of the super strike: timing a browser screenshot from outside the page to a 0.6-second slow-motion charge-up gave confusing frames, while the headless test that every super path reaches the goal answered the real question. (The opposite lesson: headless tests skip drawing, so the browser sweep that draws every moment was worth adding late; it caught the attract mode's draw-time crash.)\n\nE 0 Headless by default with exact fixed steps: the same game.advance drives unit tests, a full playthrough of the game in under two minutes and four networked copies in lockstep. And the Humanoid rig: dr\n\nE 1 A few times: E.store prefixes its keys (a test read localStorage directly), tools/check.mjs cannot take a ?query (I added a #touchonly deep link), clips cut with a SET: prefix lose it in the set, and \n\nE 2 The football itself: a match simulation (ball physics with woodwork and nets, footballers, keepers, team AI, rules, set pieces, the referee, Euphoria) with a presentation layer that only reads its sta\n\nE 3 Depth and framing in a custom 40-degree view: the goal nets, the LED board faces and the tifo had to be queued at the right anchors to sort against players, and the camera's zoom stacks the scene's st\n\nE 4 Five changes, all marked // EF: and listed in CHANGES-ENGINE.md: dialog voices that can be functions (a babble syllable per letter), named audio buses with now() and the noise buffer, football kit pat\n\nE 5 A built-in tilt or orientation channel on the Humanoid for dives, falls and acrobatics, together with kit patterns, so sports and action games do not have to patch the rig.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"452d6482-9020-4e99-982f-14f7a7dcf527","uuid":"a41af904-addb-4fb9-a2a9-67b034927ba9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T17:39:09.124Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"animation => The engine's procedural Humanoid rig (gait from velocity, attack specs for passes, shots, volleys, pokes and step-overs, poses and faces), mocap clips through Mocap.drive (slide tackles, celebrations, throws, get-ups, dodges), and a new whole-body tilt for keeper dives, diving headers, bicycle kicks, launched players and theatrical dives\n\ntesting => One command, node tools/test.mjs, 18 suites printing only failures and a count: unit physics, rules, Euphoria and super strikes, AI with a balance soak and a difficulty curve (a bot-driven human side against the CPU at Easy, Normal and Hard), content, UI by pad, keyboard, mouse and touch-only, saves, attract mode, dev mode, all nine training drills, determinism, lint, a full scripted playthrough of the cup and every mode, a fake-hub loopback with latency, jitter and loss, a browser sweep that draws every scene and moment, an audio and input check in Chromium (every kind of sound